WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.541
FEMALE SPEAKER:
Google Cloud Platform

00:00:01.541 --> 00:00:03.510
has many tools
available to Rubyists.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.920
Today I'm going to talk
to you about two of them,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.919
App Engine and the Gcloud Gem.

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:10.210
App Engine flexible environments
is our latest platform

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.464
as a service offering.

00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:13.630
With App Engine, you can
focus on writing your code.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.630
And we'll take care of
setting up the web server

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.970
and scaling your
application as needed.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.370
App Engine for Ruby supports
both Rails and Sinatra.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.995
And we have code samples for
both to help you get started.

00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:24.870
You can also use the
database of your choice,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.280
whether that's our managed MySQL
compatible Database Cloud SQL

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.550
or running your own MySQL,
Postgres, or Mongo database.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.797
To use App Engine to run
your Ruby application,

00:00:33.797 --> 00:00:36.130
start by creating a project
in the Google Cloud Platform

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.460
console and installing the
Gcloud STK on your computer.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.370
Like Rails, App
Engine uses YAML files

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.400
to configure your application.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.370
This app.yaml specifies that
we should use the Ruby runtime

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.922
and sets an entry point.

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:49.380
The entry point is
the command that

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.005
should be used to
run your application.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.050
Once you've written
your app.yaml,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.070
you can use Gcloud preview
app deploy to deploy

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.361
your application to App Engine.

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:03.150
Once the application
has deployed,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.950
you can use Gcloud
preview app browse

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.210
to launch a browser pointed
at your application.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.274
This is our app running.

00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:11.690
As you can see, the default
domain for App Engine projects

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.680
is your project ID .appspot.com.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.687
In addition to App
Engine for Ruby,

00:01:17.687 --> 00:01:19.270
there are many other
services that are

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.250
part of Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.082
The Gcloud Gem was written
by Rubyists for Rubyists.

00:01:24.082 --> 00:01:25.540
It makes interacting
with the cloud

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.260
platform products from your
Ruby applications feel natural.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.780
The Gcloud Gem supports
BigQuery, data store,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.110
cloud DNS, cloud logging,
resource manager, translate,

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.140
pub/sub, and cloud storage.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.790
Support for cloud vision and
more products is coming soon.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.910
The Gcloud Ruby
project is open source.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.380
Check it out on the Google
Cloud Platform or on GitHub.

